    "durationMs": 104408,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener API is complete and fully verified. Summary of what I built:\n\n- **`src/app.js`** — Express 5 app with all four routes: `POST /links` (201 with `{code, url, clicks: 0}`, URL echoed unchanged, auto-generated 8-char codes, 409 on duplicate custom code, 422 for malformed JSON / missing-relative-ftp URLs / invalid codes), `GET /links/:code` (200/404 JSON), `GET /r/:code` (atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1 RETURNING url` so each request increments exactly once, then 302 to the stored URL), and `GET /links` (`{items, total}` in ascending creation order).\n- **Persistence** — `pg` pool reading `DATABASE_URL` (no hard-coded credentials), a `links` table from `migrations/001_create_links.sql`, and `scripts/migrate.js` which tracks applied migrations in `schema_migrations` so `start.sh` only applies pending ones.\n- **`start.sh`** — applies migrations then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- **README** — includes the starting-the-server section.\n\nI tested the full matrix live against Postgres: every status code and JSON shape matched the spec, and after killing and restarting the server the click counts and links were still intact. Test rows were cleaned from the database afterward, so it's ready for the automated suite."
  }
